          "text": "{\"relation\":\"MATCH\",\"consensus_summary\":\"The dominant identification is that the field symbolizes the world, especially the human sphere in which the word is sown, good and evil coexist, and the final harvest occurs. Other contextual meanings are acknowledged.\",\"book_summary\":\"The field is the world, explicitly following Jesus’ interpretation, with added associations involving sowing, harvest, gleaning for the poor and stranger, and hidden treasure.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"Field = the world.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"Field = the world.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"SAME_CASES\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"No material boundary disagreement. The book adds thematic applications to gleaning and hidden treasure but does not redefine, narrow, or broaden the core referent.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"None. Both readings identify the field with the world in the controlling Matthean context.\",\"rationale\":\"The book reproduces the consensus’s minimum core identification almost verbatim and grounds it in the same explicit decoding, Matthew 13:38. Its references to sowing, harvest, gleaning, and treasure are elaborations attached to that identification rather than changes to it.\",\"citation_support\":\"PARTIAL\",\"strongest_objection\":\"Matthew 13:38 strongly and directly supports the core identification, and Leviticus 19:9–10 supports the literal gleaning detail. However, the printed citations alone do not establish that the Levitical field symbolically denotes the world, and no printed reference directly supports the hidden-treasure claim.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
